Here's an example implementation of a C++ function that takes a reference to a `std::string` containing Markdown formatted text and returns a `std::string` containing HTML formatted text:

```cpp
#include <iostream>
#include <string>
#include <unordered_map>

// Markdown to HTML conversion function
std::string markdown_to_html(const std::string& markdown) {
    // Define a mapping of Markdown syntax to HTML tags
    static const std::unordered_map<std::string, std::string> MARKDOWN_TO_HTML{
        {"# ", "<h1>"},
        {"## ", "<h2>"},
        {"### ", "<h3>"},
        {"#### ", "<h4>"},
        {"##### ", "<h5>"},
        {"###### ", "<h6>"},
        {"`", "<code>"},
        {"**", "<strong>"},
        {"*", "<em>"},
        {"[", "<a href='"],
        {"](", "')"},
        {"http(s)?://[^\s]+", "<a href='$0'>$0</a>"}
    };

    std::string html;

    // Iterate over the Markdown string and convert each syntax to its corresponding HTML tag
    for (const auto& c : markdown) {
        std::string token(1, c);
        if (MARKDOWN_TO_HTML.count(token)) {
            html += MARKDOWN_TO_HTML.at(token);
        } else {
            html += c;
        }
    }

    return html;
}

int main() {
    std::string markdown = "## Hello World\n"
                           "This is a **bold** and `code` example.\n"
                           "Visit [Google](https://www.google.com/) or https://example.com/";

    std::string html = markdown_to_html(markdown);

    std::cout << "Markdown:\n" << markdown << "\n\n";
    std::cout << "HTML:\n" << html << std::endl;

    return 0;
}
```

This function uses a static `unordered_map` to store the Markdown syntax to HTML tag mappings. It then iterates over the input `markdown` string and replaces each Markdown syntax with its corresponding HTML tag.

Note that this implementation is not a complete Markdown to HTML converter and only handles a limited set of Markdown syntax. For a more comprehensive solution, you can consider using a third-party Markdown parsing library such as [pandoc](https://pandoc.org/), [CommonMark](https://commonmark.org/), or [Markdown::Parser](https://github.com/tomav/Markdown-Parser).

